{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3452","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3452","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3452\",\"parent_revision\":\"46cc02694fcfb19f1431c2be6f0473444f3bce050a678e03419bdc565b096558\",\"root_revision\":\"46cc02694fcfb19f1431c2be6f0473444f3bce050a678e03419bdc565b096558\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-06\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA rule can be enforced in code and prescribed in prose at the same time. The two copies then drift independently, and the weaker one is often the one that blocks. Nothing detects the condition.\\n\\n## Observed instances\\n\\n**Duplicate detection.** `backlog_add` runs `find_content_duplicates` in code, and `work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` Step 3 instructs the agent to run its own concept search. Two implementations of one rule. The code copy selects terms positionally and is the blocking one: a false positive raises `DuplicateItemError` whose only escape is `force=True`, which skips the check entirely.\\n\\n**Plugin self-containment.** Already filed by hand as #3438 \u2014 `audit_runtime_escapes.py` and `tests/test_plugin_self_containment.py` check the same class independently, were written without knowledge of each other, and cover different scan populations.\\n\\n**Status vocabulary.** `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` names two tokens, `agents/task-worker.md` names three different ones, and `task_status_hook.py` accepts a third set. Filed as #3423.\\n\\nEach was found by a person noticing it. None was found by a check.\\n\\n## The required behaviour\\n\\nA script that reports where one rule has more than one enforcement point across the code and prose layers, so the condition is a finding rather than a discovery.\\n\\n## Precedent\\n\\n`audit_runtime_escapes.py` exists because \\\"reference only what ships\\\" was unenforceable as prose. The same argument applies here.\\n\\n## Correction to a related idea, verified \u2014 do not build this variant first\\n\\nThe adjacent proposal is a scan for *under-used tool surface*: list every command, flag and MCP action the code exposes, and every one no skill or agent names, on the theory that an unused capability beside a prose workaround is a finding.\\n\\nMeasured against the duplicate-detection case, that scan **would not have fired usefully**. It would have reported that `apply_search_filter` (`backlog_core/search.py:298-315`) exposes `AND`, `NOT`, regex and field scoping while no skill names them \u2014 and that is a dead end. The hand-written query that outperformed the code was `dispatch_spawn OR spawn.py OR kage-bunshin`: OR-only, the same single operator the prose template already prescribes. Grammar was never the variable; term selection was.\\n\\nSo an under-use scan is a check for a different class, and it points away from this one. Two-enforcement-point detection is the check whose absence was actually paid for.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the repo owner proposed the under-use form \u2014 \\\"a script that lists every command, flag and MCP action the tool surface exposes and every one that no skill or agent names. An unused capability beside a prose workaround is the finding.\\\" Recorded because the class is real even though it does not cover the instances above.\\n\\n## Open question for grooming\\n\\nDetecting \\\"unused\\\" is mechanical. Detecting \\\"beside a prose workaround\\\", or \\\"these two implementations enforce the same rule\\\", is not obviously mechanical at all. Whether this is a script, a review checklist item, or a rule with a manual trigger is undecided.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Repo owner, session melodic-plotting-emerson, 2026-09-06\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Open Question For Grooming\\n\\